<<~ ahu #/head>>

! The w28-gate lane ~ the dial's bytes move once

Both rows touch the sealed channel's dial bytes, so each settles before the X0 bake. `W28` froze the frame table (#/the-frozen-frames). `W10` asked whether a client can prove itself first without breaking admission or pinning (#/the-knock-reading).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-frozen-frames>>

!! The frozen frames ~ W28

|! Tag |! Name |! Direction |
| `0x01` | `presentationPart` | dialer to gate |
| `0x02` | `presentationEnd` | dialer to gate |
| `0x03` | `finish` | dialer to gate |
| `0x11` | `answerPart` | gate to dialer |
| `0x12` | `answerEnd` | gate to dialer |
| `0x13` | `verdict` | gate to dialer |

* ''The bounds'': a frame body holds at most 65518 bytes (`GATE_FRAME_BODY_MAX`), a presentation or an answer at most 1048576 bytes (`GATE_SUBJECT_MAX_BYTES`), and a finish or a verdict frame weighs 65 bytes, the tag and one Ed25519 signature (`GATE_FINISH_BYTES`, `GATE_VERDICT_BYTES`). A change to any of them takes a version rite.
* ''The reserved-tag rule'': tags `0x01` to `0x0f` belong to the dialer's direction and `0x11` to `0x1f` to the gate's. A later frame takes an unassigned tag inside its direction's block and moves no assigned tag. `0x00`, `0x10` and `0x20` to `0xff` take a version rite. Every byte outside the table reads reserved: `gateFrame`, `gateParts` and `gatePartReader` throw a `RangeError` for one, and a received reserved tag refuses.
* ''The witness'': `packages/lararium-mesh/tests/gate-frame-freeze.test.ts` pins the table, the bounds and the lengths as literals, reads all 250 unassigned bytes as reserved, and runs `gateFrameTableRefusal` against hand-built tables (a reused value, a tag outside the blocks, a gate tag in the dialer's block, a non-integer) with the real table as its known positive.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-knock-reading>>

!! The knock reading ~ W10, a research pair woken apart, two waves traded

''MEASURED'' (the code seat, the field seat, and the lead's own read of `face-gate-server.ts`): the dial already runs Noise IK with the dialer's fresh static inside message one, so a dialer already proves itself before the gate sends its verdict. A holder of a candidate responder public key alone cannot test a captured message one (it needs the `es` Diffie-Hellman). The knock (`gateKnock`, an HMAC over the bundle digest, which hashes the whole public pin) tests offline for anyone holding the full candidate pin and an observed path. Any cheap pre-filter keyed on public pin material has that property, WireGuard's `mac1` included (paper section 5.4.4); a pinned dialer holds exactly the public pin, so no filter can tell it from an observer holding the same pin. A filter an observer lacks needs a client-held secret outside the pin, which changes the admission and pinning model.

''MEASURED'': the knock also selects the gate. The dispatcher registers each Nexus and route at an exact `<route>/<knock>` path; a knock-less dial needs another clear selector or a trial decryption against every gate on a route, and each trial costs one X25519.

''BUILT, a defect apart from the leak'': `handleUpgrade` in `packages/lararium-node/src/face-gate-server.ts` once sent the HTTP 101 before the gate decrypted message one, so a holder of a learned path read a 101 for any well-formed token. It now asks `FaceGate.preflight` first: a flight that decrypts under no bundle of the stood gate draws a destroyed socket and the refusal `flight-undecrypted`, before any 101. The preflight decrypts message one once and `accept` decrypts it once more, so the gate's API keeps its one shape. It moves no wire byte.

''The open question'': W10 stays open under research (the controller, 2026-10-26). The 101 ordering stands repaired, and the question narrows to whether a sovereign-route knock can key on material the join already hands every admitted dialer.

<<~/ahu>>

